# POMPO Core Engineering Rules

POMPO is a real production fintech/payment platform.

This repository is the canonical source of truth.

## Before changing anything

- Inspect the repository and relevant files first.
- Read applicable skills and project documentation.
- Check git status and existing diffs.
- Verify whether the requested functionality already exists.
- Never duplicate an existing model, service, repository, API, or subsystem.
- Never discard existing user work.

## Architecture

Preserve the established architecture unless a concrete technical reason
requires changing it.

Backend:

```text
Routes → Dependencies → Services → Repositories → SQLAlchemy → PostgreSQL
```

Use the existing:

- FastAPI
- async SQLAlchemy
- Pydantic
- repository pattern
- service layer
- dependency injection
- Alembic
- PostgreSQL
- Redis/Celery where appropriate

Do not introduce competing architectural patterns without justification.

## Milestones

Milestones are architectural boundaries.

Before implementing a milestone:

- determine the actual current repository state
- identify what already exists
- implement only what belongs to the current milestone
- preserve completed milestones

Do not rebuild completed milestones.

Do not automatically start the next milestone after completion.

## Failure recovery

Implementation is not complete when code has merely been generated.

Always:

```text
implement → test → diagnose failures → fix legitimate failures → retest → verify → inspect diff
```

Do not:

- hide failures
- skip tests to obtain green results
- weaken assertions
- delete tests
- classify failures as pre-existing without investigation

## Security

Never hardcode:

- API keys
- passwords
- JWT secrets
- authentication tokens
- provider credentials
- webhook secrets
- private keys
- encryption keys

Use environment configuration and secure secret management.

Never expose secrets in:

- source code
- logs
- tests
- documentation
- commits
- frontend bundles

Frontend authorization is UX only.
Backend authorization is authoritative.

## Database

Never rewrite historical Alembic migrations.

Create new migrations for schema changes.

Database constraints must enforce important invariants.

## Git

Before major changes:

- inspect git status
- inspect git diff

Never perform destructive Git operations without explicit authorization.

## Completion

Never claim something passed unless it was actually executed.

Final verification should include relevant:

- tests
- migrations
- static checks
- application health
- security review
- git diff/status
